<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kovin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Keve</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kovin is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>. Known in Hungarian as Keve, it was a medieval fortress and the seat of Keve county in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, guarding a Danube crossing towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gesta-hungarorum">Gesta Hungarorum</a> names it as the stronghold of the duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glad">Glad</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kovin.jpg" alt="Kovin" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JustUser · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SrpskaCrkva.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kovin lies in the South Banat District of the autonomous province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. It is also known as Cuvin in Romanian, Kubin or Temeschkubin in German, and Kevevára in Hungarian, which until 1899 was Temeskubin. In the past it was called Donji Kovin, Lower Kovin, to tell it from a town of the same name in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, Ráckeve. The Dacian tribe of the Albocenses lived in the area in the second century CE, and there are remains of the Roman fortress of Contra Margum, opposite Margum on the other bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the ninth and tenth centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> lived here and Voivode Glad ruled the region. He was defeated by the Hungarians, and the area joined the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>. In the eleventh century a descendant of Glad, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahtum">Ahtum</a>, ruled the region and was defeated by the Hungarians too.</p><p>The earliest mention of the settlement dates from 1071, as Keuee, and in the twelfth century it was the seat of a county that included most of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a>. From the fourteenth century a large Serb population settled there, having fled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. The Serbian despot Lazar Branković took the town in 1457, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> regained it the next year.</p><p>In the sixteenth century it became part of the Ottoman Province of Temeşvar, with a mostly Serb population. In 1716 it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, and from 1751 it belonged to the Military Frontier. In 1848 and 1849 it was part of the Serbian Voivodship. When the Military Frontier was abolished in 1873 it was included in Temes county in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>.</p><p>In July 1914 a reported skirmish here was a proximate cause of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a>'s declaration of war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, but the report appears to have been false or greatly exaggerated. In 1918 Kovin joined the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>. It was under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> occupation from 1941 to 1944 and was heavily bombed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> in 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town has 11,623 inhabitants and the municipality 28,141, according to the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The municipality includes the town and villages such as Bavanište, Deliblato, Mramorak and Skorenovac. Serbs are the majority in most of the settlements, while Skorenovac has a Hungarian majority. Since 2006 Kovin has been part of an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. It is twinned with Ráckeve in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kovin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It says Glad, who came from Vidin, ruled the land between the Mureș, the Tisza and the Danube, with Cumans, Bulgarians and Vlachs, from the fortress of <b>Keve</b> (Kovin).</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-glad">Duchy of Glad</a></li></ul></section>
